 exactly how we get this  number  what if we had 18 carbon you know a  carbohydrate like glucose well we know  glucose is a six carbon molecule so if  we had three glucoses right that would  be 18 carbons right so because 3 times 6  of course is 18 so 18 carbons worth of  carbs versus 18 carbons worth of fat so  if we had 8 and 18 carbon fatty acid we  would break that down for 120 ATP how  much would we get from 18 carbons worth  of glucose right 3 lucullus molecules  well we get 3 times the amount we get  from one glucose right which is  somewhere between 32 32 ATP so that is  that at most right it would at most 96  ATP right that would be on most of  course you can get 3 times 30 which is  90 or somewhere in between that but the  most you would get is 96 which is still  less than you would get from a fatty  acid so essentially the idea here is  that fatty acids are an efficient way to  store energy now how do we actually get  fatty acids well we mentioned just above  just hear about these trays of glycerol  these triglycerides  so if we have fats right  triacylglycerols triglycerides they look  like this if you recall this portion  here in white is the glycerol backbone  and each one of these here is a fatty  acid that was attached to it if we have  trays with withdrawals we can hydrolyze  these bonds and free up fatty acids this  is this here that I've drawn here this  is a free fatty acid so if we want to  free up these fatty acids arm from a  trace of withdrawal or a triglyceride we  would have to add a particular enzyme  and those kinds of enzymes are called  lipases on the flip side these fatty  acids are also parts of phospholipids  right phospholipids are a little bit  different than triacylglycerols instead  of having a glycerol and  and three fatty acids phospholipids have  a glycerol backbone two fatty acids and  some sort of phosphate group on on it as  well  so this is specifically I've drawn  phosphatidylcholine that's not really  too important the p